• I’ve read both documents. My central understanding is that Phase 01 is not a
  disposable low-level prototype: it is meant to expose the project’s
  declarative semantics cleanly, without yet solving static typing or
  imperative-looking convenience.

  Several initial conclusions stand out.

  - Dynamic typing need not infect the execution model with exceptions. A
  single-assignment cell can resolve to either a value or a failure. Failure is
  then stable information, just like a value: readers awaken and propagate it
  rather than remaining suspended forever. Supervision can decide whether to
  replace a failed process, report the failure, or deliberately leave part of
  the computation failed. Erlang contributes useful supervision ideas, but
  ordinary “let it crash” is insufficient because this language must account for
  dependent suspended computations.
  - Ordered, oracle-free process communication should be the ordinary case. A
  linear channel naturally represents the right to append the next message, and
  each send yields its successor channel. A bag is mathematically possible as a
  collection, but choosing which available message a stateful process observes
  next becomes nondeterministic whenever that choice affects behavior. Such a
  channel therefore either needs an oracle capability or must guarantee
  confluence. That makes it valuable but not the conceptual default.
  - Replies should probably be explicit tellers, at least initially. This keeps
  process sending conceptually uniform: consume the current sending capability,
  produce the successor capability, and optionally place an answer into a
  separately supplied cell. It avoids pretending that a process interaction
  behaves like an ordinary function call.
  - The surface language can nevertheless hide successor-channel plumbing
  through static elaboration. For example, a sequence resembling Smalltalk’s
  semicolon cascade could mean “send these messages through successive states”:

  account
      withdraw: amount reply-to: answer ;
      request-balance reply-to: balance .

  This could elaborate into fresh hidden channels account₀ → account₁ →
  account₂. It would not mutate account, duplicate it, or represent one runtime
  flow multiple times. The notation would be declarative sequencing imposed by
  the linear protocol.

  - I would keep expression elaboration capable of producing multiple semantic
  components. That is a powerful bridge between pleasant notation and a linear
  execution model. A source-level name need not denote exactly one runtime
  value; it can denote a statically known bundle whose components are consumed
  separately. Eventually, the type system can make that structure explicit and
  safe.
  - I would postpone assigning universal meaning to bare juxtaposition.
  Smalltalk-style a b is attractive for immutable objects, but process messages
  introduce continuation ownership, possible tellers, blocking, and failure.
  Deciding first what a send elaborates into will make it much easier to decide
  whether a b is honest notation for it—or merely visually familiar.

  For comments, I mildly prefer # for line comments. It is conspicuous,
  economical, and especially natural in a whitespace-delimited language. Named
  symbols can use a construct that does not consume such valuable lexical
  territory. Nested /* ... */ comments also seem reasonable, though requiring
  whitespace around the delimiters would be surprising when writing prose or
  temporarily commenting out punctuation-heavy code.

  The most productive next design step seems to be defining one small process
  protocol and writing several candidate notations for:

  1. creating or obtaining its initial sending capability;
  2. sending one message;
  3. receiving a reply through a teller;
  4. sending several ordered messages;
  5. deliberately racing messages;
  6. observing a process or message failure.

  That exercise should expose whether Smalltalk-like juxtaposition and cascades
  genuinely fit the semantics.


