• Yes, that message syntax is a strong candidate to try:

  account deposit amount quantity reply ! answer .

  I would make the selector consist of the verb plus the ordered keywords. Here
  its identity would be something like:

  deposit amount reply

  The supplied flows are not part of the selector. A unary message has only its
  verb as selector.

  Including the keywords has several advantages:

  - They become semantic protocol elements rather than optional documentation.
  - Messages with the same verb but different shapes remain distinguishable.
  - Reordered arguments cannot silently call the same method incorrectly.
  - Dispatch can identify the message without inspecting argument values.
  - Protocol declarations, reflection, and later static checking get stable
  names.

  Conceptually, a message could elaborate to:

  receiver: account
  selector: [deposit amount reply]
  arguments: [quantity !answer]

  The grammar is especially clean if a message is a verb followed by zero or
  more keyword flow pairs. Nested expressions may eventually introduce
  ambiguity, but that can be addressed after the atomic-flow form has taught us
  more.

  Your proposed implicit copying rule also seems right. Multiple asker
  occurrences should invoke the structural rule of contraction automatically.
  Importantly, “copying” need not mean physically copying a resolved immutable
  value. The lower layer might:

  - fan out an unresolved flow to multiple askers;
  - share an immutable resolved representation;
  - structurally copy a representation when necessary.

  The source semantics merely promise that each asker receives equivalent
  information. An explicit copying operator would expose implementation plumbing
  and clutter ordinary declarative programs. Later, the type system can reject
  multiple asker occurrences when the flow contains linear components and
  therefore is not copyable. During the dynamically typed phase, such misuse can
  become a well-defined runtime failure.

  The teller notation is compact and fits the lexical model:

  ! answer

  I would define it specifically as a reference mode, not an operation on the
  value carried by answer:

  - answer denotes an asking reference.
  - ! answer denotes the corresponding telling reference.

  That makes the asymmetry very visible while leaving ordinary data dependencies
  pleasantly quiet. It also permits the compiler to count teller and asker uses
  independently. One remaining design question will be how a fresh flow—and
  therefore its paired rights—is introduced, but that need not alter this
  reference syntax.


