• I would use one uniform source-level notion of immutable object, while
  allowing the implementation to represent simple objects as primitive records
  when possible.

  A public component would be a unary message. Thus:

  account balance

  does not primitively extract a field. It sends the selector balance. The slot
  might contain a constant value, a flow, or computed behavior; callers cannot
  distinguish these cases. This gives you Self’s useful freedom to replace
  stored data with computation without changing client code.

  An object could semantically contain:

  - Public slots indexed by selectors.
  - Methods implementing keyword selectors.
  - Private lexical bindings captured by those methods.
  - Optionally, parent/delegation slots later.

  Private state need not be represented as specially protected public slots. It
  can simply reside in the lexical environment used while composing the object.
  A method can see it; callers cannot name it as a message selector.

  For example, without committing to punctuation:

  -object
      balance = initial-balance
      deposit amount reply = deposit-method
  /object

  Here balance is a unary selector, while deposit amount reply is a keyword
  selector. Reading a constant slot and invoking a method use the same message
  syntax.

  Runtime and compile-time composition should share an algebra but not
  necessarily denote the same kind of result:

  - Runtime composition produces a new immutable object value.
  - Compile-time composition produces or extends a program artifact from which
    values may later be constructed.

  The essential composition operation is merging selector-to-implementation
  maps. Because declarations generally run concurrently and order should not
  matter, reopening must not use Ruby’s “last definition wins” behavior. The
  merge should be commutative:

  - Disjoint selectors combine.
  - Identical repeated contributions might be accepted.
  - Conflicting definitions of the same selector are errors.
  - An explicit overriding construct, if ever added, must express precedence
    deliberately.

  That gives useful open composition without making meaning depend on source
  order.

  I would also distinguish openness from perpetual mutability. An artifact could
  collect fragments during assembly and then be sealed:

  BankAccount +: first-fragment .
  BankAccount +: deposit-fragment .
  BankAccount +: reporting-fragment .
  BankAccount seal .

  Those marks are merely illustrative. The semantic idea is that multiple
  declarations constrain one artifact, rather than successively mutating it.
  Sealing establishes the complete selector set, supports separate compilation,
  and gives hot-update versioning a definite boundary.

  There is a useful symmetry here:

  compose fragments → immutable object
  compose declarations → immutable program artifact

  The same object-body notation could be used in both contexts, with the
  surrounding binding determining the phase. I would favor that unless phase
  confusion appears in examples.

  One capability caveat is important: an object containing only copyable values
  and asker references can itself be copyable. An object whose method closes
  over a teller or another linear capability becomes linear as a whole. The
  eventual type system should derive that property structurally; during this
  dynamic phase, composition can retain runtime capability information and
  reject an illegal duplication.

  So my provisional direction would be: primitive records may exist in the
  semantic machinery, but the normal language surface exposes immutable slot
  objects, and all public observation occurs through message sending. Compile-
  time reopening then becomes order-independent composition of object fragments,
  followed by an explicit or contextual sealing boundary.

